* ============================================================================
* Section VII (new approach): Flux-noise time series generation (NO junctions)
*
* Purpose:
*   Generate δφ(t) (phase) produced by FNOISE(...) with a large, user-chosen timestep
*   without triggering the JJ timestep reduction logic.
*
* How to interpret:
*   External flux is represented as phase:
*       φ(t) = 2π Φ(t) / Φ0
*   The source PNFLUX below outputs a phase waveform δφ(t). Convert to flux:
*       δΦ(t) = (Φ0/2π) δφ(t)
*
* Output columns (CSV):
*   time, P(PNFLUX)
* ============================================================================

.option seed=123456789

* ---- Flux noise parameters (edit these) ----
.param A_PHI1  = 2.0e-6
.param ALPHA   = 1.0
.param TD      = 0.0   
.param DTNOISE = 1.0e-4
.param TCORR   = 0.0   
.param N_OU    = 48

* ---- Flux bias as phase (optional, set to 0 if you only want noise) ----
.param PHI_BIAS_FRAC = 0.25
.param PHASE_BIAS = (2*PI*PHI_BIAS_FRAC)

* Bias + noise in series so they sum cleanly
PBIAS  0    NPHI   DC    PHASE_BIAS
PNFLUX NPHI NPHI2  FNOISE(A_PHI1 ALPHA TD DTNOISE TCORR N_OU)

* tiny shunt just to keep the matrix well-behaved (phase node not totally floating)
RSHUNT NPHI2 0 1e12

* ---- Transient ----
* Choose a timestep suited to the lowest Fourier frequency you care about:
*   df_min ~ 1/Tsim  ;  Nyquist ~ 1/(2*DT)
* Example below: DT=1e-4 over 200 s -> df_min=5 mHz, Nyquist=5 kHz
.tran 1.0e-4 200 0 1.0e-4 0

.file sec7_fluxnoise.csv
.print p(PNFLUX)

.end
